data.fused_content:
Deputy Minister Phung Duc Tien remarked that the nation might still face its 16th storm of the year as 2025 draws to a close. Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department, highlighted that 2025 has experienced exceptionally atypical weather and disaster patterns, resulting in significant damage across the country. The East Sea experienced an unprecedented surge in storm and tropical depression occurrences, marking the highest activity level recorded since Vietnam's documentation began in 1961. The 2025 storm season was marked by a host of uncommon and severe events, including the emergence of Storm No. 1 (WUTIP) in the East Sea in June, an occurrence not witnessed in over four decades. The floods of 2025 were notably extensive and intense, with many rivers experiencing peak water levels that exceeded past historical highs. For the first time in a single year, 20 rivers recorded either extreme or historic flooding, particularly in the Northern and Central territories, the Central Highlands, and the lower Mekong Delta. Notably, the historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, which was once a section of Phu Yen province, surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin. The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is in the process of evaluating the 24-hour rainfall total recorded at the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue, which reached 1,739 mm in October, for possible acknowledgment as one of the greatest 24-hour rainfall totals ever documented worldwide. The pattern of intensifying weather extremes is becoming more evident, with notable variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding directly impacted by climate change.
