COLOMBO: Following the devastation wrought by a formidable cyclone, Sri Lankan officials are grappling with escalating floodwaters in sections of the capital, Colombo. According to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), the number of fatalities has risen to 159, with an additional 203 individuals unaccounted for. The water level in the Kelani River has continued to rise, causing flooding in low-lying areas. Close to 200,000 individuals have been rescued and relocated to shelters. Numerous families remain stranded in their homes due to rising floodwaters.

The severe weather conditions have led to the devastation of over 20,000 residences, displacing 122,000 individuals who now reside in government-operated temporary shelters. Over 833,000 individuals needed aid after being uprooted by the floods. Personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been dispatched to assist in the relief efforts. Access to the internet has also faced interruptions.

Although the downpours have eased throughout the island, numerous roads in the most severely impacted central province continue to be impassable. India promptly took the lead by dispatching relief supplies along with two helicopters and their respective crews for rescue operations. Japan announced its plan to dispatch a team to evaluate urgent requirements and committed to providing additional support.

Since 2017, Sri Lanka has faced its most lethal natural calamity with this cyclone, following the tragic flooding and landslides that resulted in over 200 fatalities and displaced hundreds of thousands. In June 2003, the most severe flooding since the beginning of the 21st century resulted in the loss of 254 lives.
