The Labor government plans to allocate $200 million towards 96 projects nationwide, aimed at enhancing community resilience against severe weather conditions. This includes initiatives such as an 800-person capacity cyclone shelter and evacuation center on Palm Island, off the coast of Queensland, which faces increased threats from tropical cyclones and intense storms. Additionally, a $1.6 million infrastructure project will safeguard Townsvale Estate in South Australia, and a $14.1 million investment will enhance stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera, Central NSW.

From the total $200 million allocated by the federal government, over half will be directed towards infrastructure projects with immediate construction initiation. The third round of financial support from the $1 billion Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a governmental program dedicated to enhancing disaster resilience and mitigating risks, elevates the investment to $600 million. In the past two rounds, the DRF has distributed approximately $400 million in federal funding to over 350 projects, with almost 50% of these projects currently in the pre-construction or construction-delivery stages, as reported by the department.

