In the first nine months of 2025, extreme weather events in India were linked to approximately 4,064 fatalities, according to a collaborative report by the Delhi-based Centre for Science and Environment (CSE) and Down To Earth (DTE). This figure represents a 48 percent rise over the past four years. Madhya Pradesh recorded the highest death toll, while Maharashtra suffered the most in terms of damaged agricultural land, with 8.4 million hectares affected, marking a 400 percent increase over four years. Throughout this period, the nation experienced extreme weather conditions on 99 percent of the days. In this period, at least 18 states and Union territories documented their peak count of extreme weather days since 2022. Himachal Pradesh witnessed the highest number of these events. The Climate India 2025 report, an annual publication, was unveiled on November 19, 2025, during a virtual seminar hosted by Sunita Narain. "Given the intensity and frequency of extreme weather events, it is also about what we need to do, keeping in mind that there will be more and more such disasters," she said, adding, "What we need to understand is the scale—the scale of mitigation that Belém is talking about, the scale on which the whole world has to come together."
