The World Meteorological Organization (WMO), under the United Nations, advocates for the enhancement of global monitoring and forecasting systems to warn individuals in developing countries about severe weather conditions. In her address at the UN's annual conference in Geneva, WMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo emphasized, "Early warning means early action."

The WMO notes that over the past five decades, climate-related incidents have resulted in the deaths of more than 2 million individuals. Almost 50% of countries face heightened exposure to these weather phenomena, with developing nations being especially susceptible. The agency expresses concern over inadequate defenses against these weather events and asserts that implementing early warning systems could avert most fatalities resulting from such conditions.

While advancements have been made and more nations are adopting early warning systems, significant efforts are still needed. A warning system designed for early detection provides additional time for evacuations and preparations in the event of impending extreme weather impacting a region. Such events include hurricanes, flooding, wildfires, and glacier collapses. Expanding the implementation of early warning systems has the potential to preserve lives.

The WMO is striving to raise awareness about the underuse of early warning systems for extreme weather occurrences. With the establishment of operational systems in additional countries, the preservation of lives will increase. The article highlighted ongoing advancements but emphasized the necessity for further efforts.
