It is believed to be the largest instance of illegal dumping in the UK, spanning 500 feet and reaching a depth of 20 feet. This waste 'river' was illicitly discarded in a field adjacent to the A34 and the River Cherwell near Kidlington in Oxfordshire, all at once. An MP described it as "threatening an environmental disaster," and it was discovered last month by horrified anglers. Friends of the Thames, the charity, informed the BBC: "This is an environmental catastrophe unfolding in plain sight."

The Environment Agency has initiated an investigation and urged anyone with relevant information to contact them promptly. An Environment Agency spokesperson stated: "Expert officers are currently probing the waste disposal incident near the A34 at Kidlington. We share the public's anger about incidents like this, which is why we take action against those responsible for waste crime. Anyone with information about this incident, or if they suspect waste crime elsewhere, can call the Environment Agency’s 24-hour incident hotline: [hotline number]."

Due to the considerable height of some of the waste, portions have collapsed, resulting in a distance of approximately five metres from the river. In Parliament, Calum Miller, representing Bicester and Woodstock for the Liberal Democrats, brought attention to the matter. "A massive heap of illicit plastic waste, amounting to several hundred tonnes, has been unlawfully disposed of in my constituency on a floodplain next to the River Cherwell. Water levels in rivers are climbing, and the likelihood of toxic run-off seeping into the river system grows, endangering wildlife and compromising the health of the whole catchment area."

The Environment Agency indicated that its enforcement capabilities are constrained by limited resources, noting that the projected expense of removal exceeds the total yearly budget of the local district council.
