Bangladesh has secured the 13th position in the Long-Term Climate Risk Index (CRI), facing threats like floods, heatwaves, cyclones, and storms. The CRI, compiled by Germanwatch through an analysis of data on casualties and property damage spanning the past three decades, highlights more than 9,700 incidents of extreme weather. Over the past 40 years, the number of deaths caused by cyclones in Bangladesh has decreased by over 100 times, thanks to successful risk prevention strategies and adaptive measures. The report indicates that over the past three decades, Dominica, Myanmar, and Honduras have been identified as the countries most significantly impacted. Libya, Haiti, Grenada, and China also rank within the top 10, with none of these countries being part of the affluent, industrialized nations. According to the CRI findings, nations within the Global South face heightened susceptibility to the climate crisis and require increased assistance from more affluent countries. David Eckstein, a co-author of the report, remarked, "Immediate reduction of global emissions is crucial to prevent additional human and economic damages." He added, "The pace of adaptation needs to quicken, accompanied by tangible, concrete solutions for loss and damage." In 2024, drought remained the predominant weather phenomenon, while Bangladesh, the Philippines, and India reported the highest numbers of individuals impacted by extreme weather conditions. A severe heatwave in Bangladesh, with temperatures soaring to 43.8 degrees Celsius, affected over 33 million residents. Laura Schäfer, another co-author of the report, noted, "Extreme weather events, particularly heat waves and storms, present the most significant danger to human life." She also pointed out that storms resulted in the most significant financial losses, whereas floods impacted the largest number of individuals.
