The death toll from the floods and landslides in Sumatra has risen to 174, with 116 fatalities in North Sumatra and 25 in Aceh, as reported by the National Disaster Management Agency. Additionally, 23 bodies were found in West Sumatra, according to Suharyanto, the National Disaster Mitigation Agency chief. The agency noted that thousands of homes in parts of Aceh and West Sumatra were inundated, with many submerged up to their roofs. Over 17,000 homes in West Sumatra were flooded, forcing around 23,000 people to seek refuge in temporary shelters. The floods and landslides not only destroyed rice fields, livestock, and public facilities but also severed bridges and roads, isolating residents. 

Torrential rains triggered landslides that sent mud and rocks crashing into hilly hamlets, said Achadi Subarkah Raharjo from Indonesia's Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysical Agency. The extreme weather, spurred by tropical cyclone Senyar in the Strait of Malacca, caused mudslides and rockfalls onto hilly settlements. Raharjo explained that the ongoing unstable atmospheric conditions could prolong the extreme weather while the cyclone system remains active. "We have extended the extreme weather warning due to robust water vapor supply and shifting atmospheric dynamics," Raharjo noted. The continuous downpours made the steep, saturated terrain particularly susceptible to disasters.
