COLOMBO - Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, seeking global aid as the death toll from floods and landslides caused by Cyclone Ditwah rose to 153, with 191 individuals unaccounted for. In the central district of Badulla, roads remained impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. About a third of the country was without electricity or running water due to collapsed power lines and inundated water purification facilities.

A passenger, while hospitalized, recounted how navy sailors assisted them in reaching the roof of a nearby house, using ropes to navigate through the rising floodwaters. A helicopter's initial rescue attempt was canceled as the rotor downdraft risked dislodging the roof they were on, but naval boats subsequently completed the rescue. There was no clean water available, and internet connections were also affected.

Cyclone Ditwah moved away from the island on Saturday and was heading towards neighboring India. Chennai Airport in India scrapped 54 flights due to the cyclone, with forecasts predicting intense rain and high winds for the next 48 hours. The Sri Lankan government has called for international aid and encouraged Sri Lankans overseas to contribute financially to support impacted communities. An Indian warship, on a pre-scheduled goodwill visit to Colombo, donated its supplies to assist the victims.
