Unrelenting extreme weather has battered North Sumatra for several consecutive days, inundating sections of the Tapanuli Selatan district beginning on Monday, as reported by the national disaster agency, BNPB.

In Tapanuli Selatan, the calamity of floods and landslides has led to the tragic loss of eight lives, injuries to 58 individuals, and the displacement of 2,851 residents who were forced to evacuate. According to Abdul Muhari, spokesperson for BNPB, the local disaster agency has dispatched heavy machinery to remove debris that has obstructed road access to the district. Flooding and landslides have also impacted at least three additional districts within the province.

The yearly monsoon period, usually spanning from June to September, frequently results in intense rainfall, which can cause landslides, flash flooding, and the spread of waterborne illnesses. The effects of climate change have altered storm patterns, affecting both the length and intensity of the season, which results in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and intensified wind gusts. Approximately 13 individuals remain unaccounted for.
