JAKARTA Severe storms have caused the death toll to rise above 400 across three Indonesian provinces on Sumatra, with 290,000 individuals displaced. Southern Thailand, northern Malaysia, and Vietnam have similarly suffered from severe flooding, largely attributed to climate change. North Sumatra, West Sumatra, and Aceh faced substantial rainfall and landslides since November 25 due to Cyclone Senyar and Cyclone Koto. Landslides have obstructed access routes in highland regions, hindering both evacuations and the delivery of aid. Suhariyanto, the head of Indonesia's National Disaster Mitigation Agency (BNPB), stated that certain areas remain inaccessible and that extended power outages and disrupted communications were further hindering rescue and relief efforts. They have supplied electric generators and Starlink units to local authorities, shelters, and military and police personnel. The unstable weather patterns and soil conditions pose significant difficulties, according to Hery Marantika, who leads the Search and Rescue Office in Medan. Tropical cyclones have seen an increase in frequency over the last decade, typically occurring from November until March or April of the subsequent year, as noted by Dwikorita Karnawati, Chairwoman of the Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency (BMKG). Land cover in the upstream and riverbank areas of Central Tapanuli has significantly declined, mainly due to deforestation for the Batang Toru hydropower project, local logging operations, and gold mining activities. Although the storms have impacted three provinces, collectively housing over 20 million residents, the central government has refrained from announcing a national disaster. The central government has dispatched three Hercules C-130 aircraft alongside an A-400 aircraft, all loaded with emergency provisions for the residents impacted by the disaster.
