A lightning flash stretching 515 miles, captured by U.S. satellite imagery, has surpassed the previous record-holder, which was believed to have spanned 477 miles across Texas, Louisiana, and Mississippi. This finding emerged while reviewing satellite imagery of the 2017 storm, and these lightning bursts were classified as "megaflashes" due to their extremely long duration and distance.

According to the World Meteorological Organization (WMO), a car would require around eight to nine hours, while a commercial airplane would need at least 90 minutes to traverse this distance. Professor Randall Cerveny from Weather and Climate Extremes at WMO highlighted that progress in scientific and technological fields enables the evaluation of extreme weather phenomena, such as measuring lightning distance. "It is likely that even greater extremes still exist," Cerveny remarked.

Images of both megaflashes were taken in 2017 and 2020 as part of the Geostationary Operational Environmental Satellite (GOES) program. GOES-16, launched in 2016, is a component of the GOES-R satellite series, which comprises four satellites.

NOAA and NASA collaborate to observe and analyze lightning, enhancing weather understanding and forecasting abilities. The United States experiences approximately 25 million cloud-to-ground lightning strikes each year. Lightning is associated with 24,000 fatalities and 240,000 injuries. The National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) within NOAA monitors the financial impact of extreme weather events, which amounts to billions of dollars. In 2024, they documented 27 significant natural disasters, comprising 17 severe storms such as tornado outbreaks, high winds, and hailstorms, alongside 5 tropical cyclones and 2 winter storm/cold waves. These 27 events collectively incurred costs totaling $182.7 billion, following a record 28 separate billion-dollar events in 2023. Meteorological forecasts predict that intensified weather phenomena, along with rising temperatures and shifting climate patterns, will lead to stronger storms.
