Temperatures can drop to below -30 degrees, with heavy snowfall enveloping the landscape. Locally referred to as the 'white death', a dzud is a severe climatic phenomenon in Mongolia, occurring when a summer drought precedes a brutal winter. The winter of 2022-2023 was exceptionally harsh, with 90% of the country's land covered in thick snow, marking it as the heaviest snowfall in 49 years. This dzud claimed millions of livestock, not only from the severe cold but also from malnutrition, as droughts over the summer months had severely limited food cultivation. Regrettably, the herding communities bear the most severe consequences, often losing their livelihoods as many families rely on selling their produce to sustain themselves.

The dzud is not merely a natural catastrophe but also a socio-economic crisis. As the snow eventually melts, the resulting water triggers widespread flooding throughout the country, further devastating infrastructure and grazing areas, while heightening the risk of food and waterborne illnesses, especially in impoverished regions with rudimentary sanitation systems.

Ongoing intervention efforts and aid from organizations like the WHO, World Bank, and Mongolian Red Cross Society have been crucial. Frontline health workers with medical supplies and the capacity to offer psychological support to those affected have been offering aid in the 21 affected provinces. Weather data gathered is used to trigger the release of funding ahead of catastrophic weather events, ensuring herding families are protected with financial assistance before the dzud even begins. It has continued to help herder households get back on their feet, delivering critical humanitarian assistance where needed and offering services such as psychological support to the families who suffered the hardest hit.

Looking ahead, the efforts of organizations like the WHO, World Bank, and Mongolian Red Cross Society show that early intervention, social protection programs, and community support can make a difference.
