data.fused_content:
The Albanese government’s refusal to declare South Australia’s algal bloom a natural disaster mirrors Scott Morrison’s weak justification for not being the one holding the hose during the Black Summer bushfires of 2019-20. Thousands of kilometers of South Australian coastline are now strewn with lifeless sharks, rays, fish, dolphins, and seals, severely impacting both tourism and the fishing sector. Early pleas for aid were made by the South Australian government, scientists, and environmental organizations, but Canberra was preoccupied with the federal election until this week. Federal Environment Minister Murray Watt finally revealed a $14 million aid package, though it still does not classify the situation as a natural disaster. According to the Commonwealth's natural disaster framework, phenomena such as floods, cyclones, and bushfires are categorized as natural disasters, and once officially recognized, these events become eligible for various funding options. Researchers suggest the bloom's emergence may be attributed to a mix of nutrient-rich water from 2022 floods traversing the Murray-Darling system, the ongoing drought in South Australia, and a marine heatwave last September that elevated sea temperatures by 2.5 degrees beyond the usual levels. The algal bloom, induced by the microalgae Karenia mikimotoi, has extended from the Fleurieu Peninsula to the Yorke Peninsula and into the ecologically vulnerable Coorong, traversing the mouth of the Murray River. Descending 20 meters below the ocean's surface, the algal bloom has swelled to nearly twice the size of the ACT and may be the biggest to hit Australia’s coast. Although the financial support is appreciated, investigating the algal bloom within the Great Southern Reef system at the southernmost part of Australia presents a costly scientific challenge, primarily due to the considerable depth of the waters involved. There is also apprehension that the prevailing currents might transport the bloom into the waters of Victoria or Western Australia.
