data.fused_content:
Intense rainfall throughout the past week has inundated southern and central Taiwan, causing floods, landslides, and road closures, severely damaging properties and prompting the evacuation of thousands. The continuous rain forced the closure of schools and offices in certain regions throughout the week. During last month's heated mass recall campaigns, a typhoon struck, prompting visits to the affected areas by the president, premier, and local government officials. Chiayi Mayor Huang Min-hui (黃敏惠) of the KMT expressed gratitude towards Kaohsiung Mayor Chen Chi-mai (陳其邁) of the DPP for quickly dispatching personnel to assist Chiayi City with relief efforts. Meanwhile, KMT Legislator Niu Hsu-ting (牛煦庭) criticized the sharing of photos depicting Taiwan Power Co workers repairing utility poles post-typhoon, labeling it as "emotional blackmail" of voters, especially since KMT lawmakers had previously reduced a subsidy for the utility.

The storm's fierce winds and heavy rainfall led to agricultural losses exceeding NT$2.6 billion (US$86.6 million), while also wreaking havoc on over 23,000 roofs and toppling a record-breaking nearly 2,500 utility poles, resulting in widespread power failures. Several weather stations reported rainfall surpassing 100mm per hour, notably recording 122mm per hour in Pingtung County’s Sandimen Township. The CWA characterized rainfall exceeding 80mm per hour as "creating a sensation akin to pressure or suffocation, as though the sky is falling; surpassing most urban drainage systems' capacities; and can readily trigger floods and landslides." This intensity is confined to severe weather events, and for many, it might be a once-in-a-lifetime occurrence.

The Central Weather Administration (CWA) shared a chart on Facebook illustrating the intensity of the brief yet intense rainfall that affected southern Taiwan. This chart connected hourly rainfall statistics with "common perceptions" to aid public comprehension of various rainfall intensities. For instance, it equated less than 0.1mm of rain per hour to “the refreshing mist from water sprayers at summer amusement parks.” The CWA's initiative to inform the public about scientific facts using clear and straightforward language exemplifies impartial public communication. It is essential for the government to address this issue earnestly and devise clear and swift methods to communicate facts and policies effectively, aiming to reduce the dissemination of damaging misinformation, not only from opposition groups but also from China.
