An unprecedented algal bloom, detected near the Fleurieu Peninsula in March, has extended along the shoreline, claiming the lives of tens of thousands of marine creatures. This environmental catastrophe has prompted a collaborative $28 million investment package from both the South Australian and federal governments, aimed at clean-up, recovery, research, and industry support. The spread of the algal bloom has led to a dense layer of foam on beaches, typically stunning, and fish, dolphins, rays, and sharks washing up dead. 

Labor, the coalition, and the Greens have backed a Senate inquiry into the matter, which received approval on Wednesday night. Nationals senator Ross Cadell remarked, "Whether it be flood, drought, bushfire, or algal bloom, our communities need support, not politics." The inquiry provides an opportunity to deeply understand the tangible consequences of this ecological catastrophe and evaluate the adequacy of the existing disaster relief funding framework. 

