On August 26, Jammu Division faced an extraordinary natural disaster due to rainfall breaking a 99-year-old record, causing widespread destruction. Many lives were lost, including those of pilgrims at the Shri Mata Vaishno Devi shrine, with numerous injuries and hundreds made homeless. Infrastructure worth crores was destroyed. The landslide disaster on the Vaishno Devi pilgrimage path transformed what should have been a sacred journey into a horrifying ordeal. Extensive construction projects along the route disrupted the natural landscape, leading to unforeseen ecological consequences.

The Chenab and Tawi rivers surged over 10 feet above the danger threshold, causing road and bridge collapses and leaving thousands of vehicles stranded. Despite advance warnings from the Meteorological Department, preventive actions were lacking. The shrine board and administration were aware of the Trikuta hills' susceptibility but failed to take necessary precautions. The pilgrimage could have been halted earlier to ensure safety, and pilgrims could have been sheltered in Katra, where temporary facilities are available.

