A report has revealed that since 2020, the UK has lost a year's supply of bread due to harvests being devastated by severe weather conditions, including drought and floods. Extreme weather events, intensified by climate change, have resulted in a shortfall of more than 7 million tonnes in wheat output. This decade has witnessed two of the top three poorest harvests in history, with the most severe year being 2020, followed by 2024. The climate crisis introduces unpredictability for farmers, causing various growers to face alternating periods of prosperity and decline.

Colin Chappell, an arable farmer from Lincolnshire, commented, "I have never known a more challenging time to farm. As a farmer, I am used to managing the worst the weather has to throw at me, but recent years have become impossible. I require additional assistance to adapt to these extremes and build the resilience of my business."

Specialists at the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) estimated that this quantity of wheat could produce over 4 billion loaves of bread, equating to a year's worth of supply. The situation is anticipated to deteriorate further as climate change induces extended spells of drought alongside intensified, severe rainfall, potentially leading to floods. Millers are bringing in unprecedented quantities of wheat to produce flour for bread, cakes, and biscuits, with last autumn's imports being twice the five-year average.

Farming amid shifting climates reveals significant challenges, diminishing our ability to independently produce our primary staple crop. The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024. Apple orchards have experienced an exceptionally fruitful season, while winegrowers predict 2025 will be a standout year for vintages due to abundant sunny days.

"This is what farming in a changing climate looks like," said Tom Lancaster, an analyst specializing in land, food, and farming at the ECIU. "Adverse weather conditions are diminishing the British nature of our bread, as millers increasingly rely on imports to compensate for reduced UK production, a trend expected to worsen with the escalation of extreme weather in the years to come."

Colin Chappell added, "Getting green farming initiatives reopened has to be a priority if farmers are to sustain the cultivation of wheat and other essential crops for the Great British public."
