After the earthquake on Sunday, authorities warned that even though any resulting waves would likely be minimal, they could still pose a risk to those watching from the shore. Residents were cautioned to steer clear of the coast. "Although the anticipated wave heights are minimal, you must still distance yourself from the shore," stated Russia's Ministry for Emergency Services on Telegram on Sunday. The Ministry for Emergency Services of Russia also indicated that the tremor near the Kuril Islands might have generated a tsunami wave reaching a height of 19 centimeters (7.5 inches). There has been no tsunami warning announced for Hawaii or any other part of the United States.

The German Research Center for Geosciences first assessed the earthquake as having a magnitude of 6.35, but subsequently revised it to 6.7. Local authorities initially alerted that three regions faced potential impacts before the advisories were revoked. "Due to seismic activity originating in the Pacific Ocean region, the Aleutsky municipal district might experience a tsunami wave that reaches a maximum height of 19 centimeters," according to the Kamchatka Tsunami Monitoring and Warning Center. Since then, the Main Directorate of the Ministry of Emergency Situations of Russia for Kamchatka Krai announced via Telegram: "The tsunami threat to Kamchatka's coasts has been lifted. There is no verified evidence of a tsunami wave nearing the region's settlements."

