Hurricane Beryl, the first major hurricane of the 2024 Atlantic season and the earliest category 5 hurricane on record, impacted Barbados as a category 3 storm, causing large swells and waves along the south and west coasts. While the storm resulted in substantial damage to coastal assets, it did not trigger a humanitarian emergency. The total impact was valued at 193 million Barbados dollars, representing 0.15% of the nation’s GDP. Damage accounted for 58% of the total impact, followed by losses at 36%, and additional expenses at 5%. The sectors of tourism, fisheries, agriculture, and the environment collectively represented 84% of these impacts. The early emergence of this hurricane underscores the growing unpredictability of climate patterns and highlights the need to enhance disaster risk management, including risk assessment, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, and response.
