In late October 2023, the town of Rochester, situated along the Campaspe River, experienced one of its most severe floods on record. Floodwaters inundated a thousand homes, causing extensive damage. Leigh Wilson, chairperson of the Rochester Community Recovery Committee, notes that while the community has made a strong recovery, numerous individuals have faced significant challenges with their insurance providers over the past three years, particularly with rising insurance premiums. Wilson emphasizes that enhancing access to insurance could greatly benefit many communities.

The Insurance Council of Australia reports that the financial toll from three major disasters this year has reached nearly $2 billion. Their latest report on insurance catastrophe resilience highlights how climate change is intensifying weather events and their economic impacts. The council advocates for establishing a disaster preparedness fund to bolster essential infrastructure. Kylie Macfarlane, deputy chief executive of the Insurance Council of Australia, underscores that Australia's distinct geographical features increase vulnerability to natural disasters.

Greg Mullins, founder of Emergency Leaders for Climate Action and former Commissioner for Fire and Rescue New South Wales, asserts that investing a dollar in resilience can save up to $10 by reducing the costs associated with response and recovery efforts. He emphasizes the need to safeguard communities promptly. The report also calls for enhanced land use strategies and targeted investments to strengthen resilience against weather-related events.
