Mrutyunjay Mohapatra, the Director of IMD, indicated that although light rain will persist throughout the coming week, there is no forecast for heavy, widespread rainfall in the short term. He highlighted the potential emergence of a new low-pressure system in the Bay of Bengal around September 20, noting that there is a chance of wind gusts developing, though a cyclone is improbable at this time. The forecast is expected to provide more clarity in the days ahead.

Although the overall rainfall is diminishing, the IMD cautions that localized heavy showers and thunderstorms remain a threat, especially in regions susceptible to flooding and low-lying zones. The earlier low-pressure system, which caused heavy rainfall in the coastal and western districts, has now moved towards north Telangana and Vidarbha, resulting in a steady decrease in rainfall throughout the state.

Authorities remain vigilant, keeping a watchful eye on the Bay of Bengal for any potential events that might affect both coastal and inland areas. No imminent threat of cyclones or severe weather conditions to Odisha is anticipated.
