data.fused_content:
TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire consumed homes in a California Gold Rush town, originally established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. The blaze swiftly grew to encompass 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of the town of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency. No initial reports indicated any injuries or fatalities.

By Wednesday morning, the fire had expanded to cover over 19 square miles, according to CalFire. Among several blazes known as the TCU September Lightning Complex, this fire has consumed over 19 square miles across various areas. Fire authorities have sought extra assistance, including fire trucks, bulldozers, and a plane.

On Tuesday evening, a group of seven individuals made a desperate attempt to keep the blaze from spreading from the house next door. Their efforts lasted approximately 30 minutes until the firefighters arrived. An RV on the property was damaged by the fire.

Chinese Camp originated when Chinese miners established themselves there after being expelled from a neighboring camp, as stated by Visit Tuolumne County. Initially named Camp Washington, it was later renamed to honor the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers. Chinese miners faced widespread discrimination and hostility during the Gold Rush era, including the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to force them out of the mining industry.
