The Environment Agency has completed 151 flood management projects throughout England over the year since Labour took office in July last year. These initiatives, which include the construction of new defenses and upgrades to existing ones, have significantly enhanced flood protection for over 24,000 homes and businesses nationwide. The Environment Agency has completed several notable schemes, including the Saltfleet to Gibraltar Point Beach Management scheme in Lincolnshire, which protects 2,904 local properties, and the Cockett Wick seawall improvement scheme in Jaywick, which safeguards 1,526 properties. Additionally, the £75 million Canvey Island Flood Defence Scheme, which renewed three kilometers of tidal defenses in Essex, was completed in July, better protecting more than 6,000 properties for the next 50 years.

The Government has pledged to invest £2.65 billion in flood defense spending over the next two years until April 2026, marking a £250 million increase over the previous Tory government's allocation. The Government has also committed to spending £7.9 billion on flood defenses from 2026 to 2036, bringing the total to more than £10.5 billion over the 12 years from 2024. Caroline Douglass, executive director of floods and coastal risk management at the Environment Agency, stated, "Our teams and partners have worked diligently to implement these crucial flood schemes nationwide, ready to protect people and homes this winter." She added, "This investment is set to benefit more than 890,000 properties, protecting businesses, safeguarding farmland, and allowing the Government to push ahead with its plans to build 1.5 million new homes by 2029. It will also help our towns and cities to prosper by driving forward economic growth, creating new jobs, and building new homes for future generations."
